I now have a solid understanding of the existing Banking implementation (backend job/statement pipeline, allocation/reallocation mechanics, LLM abstraction, dedup/fingerprinting, Flutter nav/state patterns) to properly evaluate this plan. Overall the plan is well-structured and mostly reuses the right patterns, but there are several real gaps/risks worth resolving before implementation starts. I'm not blanket-approving — here's the review.

Verdict: Not yet — solid plan, but 5 issues need a decision before coding starts
✅ What's genuinely reusable (confirmed against actual code)
nextId() BigInt PK generation, raw-SQL migration convention (data_model/*.sql + tsx src/scripts/migrate.ts), controller/service/repository split, zod validation, HttpError, AuthedRequest middleware — all directly reusable as described.
statement_jobs-style job table + polling (UPLOADING→PROCESSING→VALIDATING→SAVING→COMPLETED/FAILED/AWAITING_CONFIRMATION/REJECTED) is a great fit for the Investments upload job.
callLlm() in llmService.ts is a clean text-in/text-out abstraction — reusable as-is regardless of source file type.
The "derived aggregate row updated transactionally via delta" pattern (account_budget_allocations + recalcBudgetAggregates) is the right model to mirror for investment_positions / investments_pot_positions.
⚠️ Issues to resolve before implementation
1. Asset-level Pot "reallocation" doesn't map cleanly onto Banking's reallocation UX (biggest gap). Banking's budget_reallocation_sheet.dart works because account_budget_allocations.amount is a single mutable aggregate row per (account, budget) — you can freely increase/decrease it. In Investments, the plan states investment_transaction_pot_allocations (per-transaction, percentage-based) is the source of truth, and investments_pot_positions is a derived read model. So when a user does "reallocate this Asset's holding between Pots" at the Account+Asset level (not the single-trade level), which of the potentially many underlying BUY/SELL trades' allocation_value rows actually change? The plan doesn't specify this. Two coherent options:

(a) Asset-level reallocation overwrites the pot-split uniformly across all of that asset's transactions going forward (simple, deterministic, recomputable) — my suggestion.
(b) Directly edit the aggregate investments_pot_positions.units like Banking does — but this breaks the "positions are derived from transaction-level allocations" invariant and would diverge from source-of-truth on any future recompute.
This needs an explicit decision before writing the reallocation sheet — "follow existing Banking allocation/reallocation UX as closely as applicable" isn't sufficient guidance here since the underlying data shape is fundamentally different.

2. SELL-trade allocation semantics are undefined. The plan treats every unlabeled trade (BUY or SELL) the same way in the labeling flow (assign % across Pots), but never states: does a SELL's allocation subtract allocated_units from the Pot(s) it's assigned to? What happens if a SELL is allocated to a Pot that doesn't have enough previously-allocated units to cover it (drift from parsing quirks, or an earlier BUY that was never allocated)? This needs a validation rule (e.g. reject/flag if a Pot's resulting position would go negative) — Banking's "budgets can go negative by design" rule shouldn't be assumed to carry over here since negative units in a Pot isn't a meaningful state the way a negative budget is.

3. LLM-parsing structured Excel data is a different risk profile than Banking's PDF-OCR case. Banking uses the LLM because PDF layout/OCR is genuinely ambiguous. Trade Books/Holdings statements are already tabular (rows/columns) — an LLM misreading a single digit in price or amount is a real risk, and critically, the described reconciliation only validates units, not price/amount. A price/amount hallucination on one row would sail through reconciliation undetected since units still tie out. I'd suggest either (a) keeping LLM-only per your instruction but adding a per-row amount ≈ units × price sanity check as an extra guard, or (b) reconsidering deterministic Excel parsing (which is actually easier than Banking's PDF case, since there's no OCR ambiguity — just generic header-name matching). Flagging this as a decision point rather than overriding your explicit instruction to use LLM-only for v1.

4. Reconciliation dummy trade needs a defined price/amount source, and its own fingerprint. investment_transactions.price/amount are presumably NOT NULL. When a per-asset unit mismatch is detected and a dummy reconciliation trade is proposed, what price gets stored on it? (Options: the Holdings file's reported NAV/price for that asset, if present — explicitly not written to investments_asset_latest_prices, just used locally for this row.) Also, unlike Banking's single scalar delta → one dummy transaction, Investments reconciliation is per-asset, so there could be multiple dummy trades in one job — the AWAITING_CONFIRMATION payload shape and confirm/reject controller logic need genuine redesign (array of per-asset mismatches, not Banking's single delta/dummyType), not literal reuse.

5. Bottom-nav wiring needs real changes, not just relabeling. Currently in home_screen.dart, _onNavTap buttons 1 ("Transactions") and 2 ("+") unconditionally push Banking's TransactionsScreen, regardless of the _isBanking switcher (which today only toggles the Home tab's body). To make "Trades" replace "Transactions" for Investments as described, _onNavTap must branch on _isBanking and push an Investments-specific screen instead. Small, but worth calling out explicitly as a required change rather than an "existing structure, just adapt labels" item.

Minor gaps worth a quick decision (not blockers)
No xlsx/Excel-parsing library exists in backend/package.json today — adding one is a legitimate, minimal new dependency, just flagging it since the guidelines say "avoid unnecessary dependencies" and I want that addition pre-approved rather than assumed.
ISIN-only asset identification + "reject upload if missing ISIN" — trade books often include non-trade ledger lines (STT, stamp duty, DP charges) with no ISIN. Need an explicit rule for filtering those out before the "no ISIN → reject whole upload" check fires.
status columns on investment_pots/investment_accounts/etc. are a new convention — Banking's accounts/budgets tables have no status column today. Fine to introduce, just pick the enum values explicitly (e.g. ACTIVE/ARCHIVED) since there's no existing precedent to mirror.
tigzig NAV API: response has isin and isin2 — matching logic should check both; also scheme_code returned by this third-party API may not be the authoritative AMFI code — recommend not writing it into amfi_scheme_code unless verified, treat it as a response-matching key only.
Single-account-per-upload assumption (Holdings + Trade Book both scoped to one selected account) should be explicitly confirmed — consolidated broker statements sometimes span multiple demat accounts in one file.
None of this contradicts your architecture — it's all solvable within the model you've defined. I'd just get explicit answers on #1–#4 (especially #1) before code is written, since they affect the shape of investment_transaction_pot_allocations usage and the reconciliation job payload, which everything else builds on.

